<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Zidan Abu Maali</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1627">1627</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Mulay Zidan, Moulay Zidan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Zidan Abu Maali, known as Mulay Zidan, was a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> and Saadi sultan of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1603 to 1627. After his father's death he fought his brothers for the throne in a long civil war, eventually holding <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> while rivals and religious leaders controlled other regions. His library, seized by Spanish ships in 1612, ended up in the Escorial, where it survives.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Zidan was the son and heir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ahmad-al-mansur">Ahmad al-Mansur</a> by his wife Lalla Aisha bint Abu Bakkar, a lady of the Chebanate tribe. When his father died in 1603 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> fell into anarchy as the sultan's sons fought for the throne. His brother Mohammed esh Sheikh el Mamun took the northern half of the country, and Zidan was left to rule only the south, while a plague epidemic killed a third of the population.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>Zidan lost much of his authority to warring factions and insubordinate governors. Warlords rose against him, among them Ahmed ibn Abi Mahalli in the south and Sidi al-Ayachi in the north. When el Mamun ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/larache">Larache</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1610, the Spanish also seized al-Ma'mura, and the religious standing of the Saadians as defenders of the faith suffered. The hardest blow came from Abi Mahalli, who claimed to be the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tafilalt">Tafilalt</a> and the Draa, and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marrakesh">Marrakesh</a> in 1612, forcing Zidan to flee. The Treaty of London of 1604, which ended the Anglo-Spanish war, freed the Spanish navy to raid the Moroccan coast, and one of his governors set up the independent Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sale">Salé</a>.</p><p>Abroad, Zidan resumed the tribute to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> to protect himself from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algiers">Algiers</a>. Through envoys such as Samuel Pallache he made a Treaty of Friendship and Free Commerce with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> in 1609, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James I of England</a> sent John Harrison to him in 1610, 1613 and 1615 to obtain the release of English captives.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Zidan died in September 1627. Part of his library outlived him in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. During Abi Mahalli's revolt he hired a French privateer, Jehan Philippe de Castelane, to carry his household goods from Safi to Santa Cruz do Cabo for 3,000 escudos. Unpaid after six days, Castelane sailed for Marseille with the cargo, but ships of the Spanish admiral Luis Fajardo intercepted him near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mehdya">Mehdya</a>. The books went by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lisbon">Lisbon</a> and Cádiz to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> and in 1614 to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/el-escorial">El Escorial</a>. The collection, some 4,000 books and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>, is one of the most important bodies of Arabic manuscripts in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Zidan Abu Maali” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
